```java
public boolean exists () {
    switch (type) {
    case Internal:
        if (file().exists()) return true;
        // Fall through.
    case Classpath:
        return FileHandle.class.getResource("/" + file.getPath().replace('\\', '/')) != null;
    }
    return file().exists();
}
```

So for Internal type, `exists()` checks the filesystem first, and if not found, falls through to classpath check. 

And `read()`:
```java
public InputStream read () {
    if (type == FileType.Classpath || (type == FileType.Internal && !file().exists()) || ...) {
        InputStream input = FileHandle.class.getResourceAsStream("/" + file.getPath().replace('\\', '/'));
        ...
    }
    ...
}
```

So `read()` for Internal falls back to classpath when the file doesn't exist on the filesystem. 

So as long as the properties files are on the classpath (via `sourceSets.main.resources.srcDirs += ['assets']`), both `exists()` and `read()` will find them via classpath fallback. The standard pattern works.

